Interview Questions

Q: Explain the Central Limit Theorem.
A: The CLT states that, under fairly general conditions, the distribution of the sample mean (when properly normalized) approaches a normal distribution as sample size grows.
   It allows us to apply normal-based inference (e.g. confidence intervals, z-tests) even when raw data aren’t normal.

Q: What is a p-value?
A: In hypothesis testing, the p-value is the probability of obtaining results at least as extreme as the observed one, assuming the null hypothesis is true. 
   A small p-value suggests the observed result is unlikely under the null, leading to its rejection.

Q: How do variance and standard deviation differ?
A: Variance is the average squared deviation from the mean; standard deviation is its square root. 
   Standard deviation has the same units as the data and is often easier to interpret, but variance is used in many formulas because of its algebraic properties.

Q: What does Pearson’s correlation coefficient measure?
A: It measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two variables, with values between –1 (perfect negative) and +1 (perfect positive). 
   For example, a correlation of 0.8 indicates a strong positive linear trend.

Q: What’s the difference between one-tailed and two-tailed tests?
A: In a one-tailed test, the alternative hypothesis specifies a direction (e.g. mean > 0), so we only look at one end of the distribution for significance. 
   In a two-tailed test, we consider deviations on both ends (mean ≠ 0), effectively doubling the critical p-value region.

Q: When would you use ANOVA?
A: ANOVA is used to compare the means of three or more groups. It tests whether there is a statistically significant difference among group means.
   For example, ANOVA can test if average sales differ across multiple regions.

